    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nThe v-t graph forms a trapezoid. Area of trapezoid = (1/2) \u00d7 (sum of parallel sides) \u00d7 height.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nt\u2081 = 10 s, t\u2082 = 20 s (constant speed), t\u2083 = 10 s. Total time = 40 s. Constant speed duration = 20 s. Total distance = 600 m.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nArea = (1/2) \u00d7 (b\u2081 + b\u2082) \u00d7 v_max\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. The lower base of the trapezoid is total time: b\u2081 = 10 + 20 + 10 = 40 s.\n2. The upper base is the duration of constant velocity: b\u2082 = 20 s.\n3. Area = (1/2) \u00d7 (40 + 20) \u00d7 v_max = 600 m.\n4. (1/2) \u00d7 60 \u00d7 v_max = 30 \u00d7 v_max = 600 m.\n5. v_max = 600 / 30 = 20 m/s.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption B: 20 m/s\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nTrapezoid area formula saves massive calculation time in 3-stage motion problems: Area = 1/2 \u00d7 (t_total + t_const) \u00d7 v_max.",
