    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nBall 2 has u = \u221a(2gH). Meeting time t = H / (u_rel) = H / \u221a(2gH) = \u221a(H / 2g). Ball 1 falls y = 1/2 g t\u00b2 = 1/2 g (H / 2g) = H/4. Height above ground = H - H/4 = 3H/4.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nBall 1 dropped from H. Ball 2 thrown from ground with u = \u221a(2gH).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nt_meet = H / u; y\u2081 = (1/2) g t\u00b2; h_meet = H - y\u2081\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Ball 2 can just reach height H, so its initial velocity is u = \u221a(2gH).\n2. Since both experience downward acceleration g, their relative acceleration is a_rel = g - g = 0.\n3. Relative speed between them is constant: v_rel = u + 0 = \u221a(2gH).\n4. Time taken to meet: t = H / v_rel = H / \u221a(2gH) = \u221a(H / 2g).\n5. Distance fallen by the dropped ball in this time:\n   y\u2081 = (1/2) g t\u00b2 = (1/2) g [H / (2g)] = H / 4.\n6. Height of meeting point above the ground: h = H - y\u2081 = H - H/4 = 3H/4.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption B: 3H / 4\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nRelative motion shortcut: Since relative acceleration is ZERO under gravity, meeting time is simply t = Separation / Relative Speed!",
